+
+### macOS background computer control
+
+The `computer` tool supports a separate light blue virtual pointer (`#7DD4FF`) for
+window-targeted macOS input. It turns darker blue (`#2563EB`) while the left
+button is pressed or dragging, then returns to light blue on release. Call `{"action":"windows"}` first, choose a window,
+then pass its `window_id` and `pid` on screenshots and input actions. The same tool
+is available through the CLI, MCP, normal agents, and Light mode. Computer control
+must be enabled in Settings; macOS Accessibility and Screen Recording permissions
+are still required.
+
+```json
+{"action":"screenshot","window_id":123,"pid":456}
+{"action":"click","window_id":123,"pid":456,"x":440,"y":194,"screen_width":2000,"screen_height":1500}
+{"action":"type","window_id":123,"pid":456,"text":"Hello from the background"}
+{"action":"drag","window_id":123,"pid":456,"path":[[500,220],[630,618],[291,372],[709,372],[370,618],[500,220]],"duration":3}
+```
+
+Take one explicit screenshot for the initial view. After every successful move,
+click, drag, type, key, or scroll, Lessagent waits two seconds and captures the
+same target window automatically before returning the tool result. Agent and
+Light mode captures go into the current `agent/output` iteration; direct calls
+use `agent/output/computer`. MCP returns a PNG image content block containing
+base64, alongside the saved path and dimensions. The next model request receives
+the observation, so it does not need to issue a separate screenshot action.
+Desktop controls similarly capture the desktop. Control and observation are
+serialized so concurrent tool calls cannot interleave during the settling delay.
+If capture fails after input, the result preserves input success and reports
+`screenshot_error`; capture again rather than repeating the input blindly.
+
+Replace the example IDs with discovery results. Coordinates start at the target
+window's top-left, including its title bar. Without screenshot dimensions they
+are logical macOS points. With dimensions they are pixels of that window's
+screenshot. A path draws one continuous stroke with a single button-down/up pair;
+all points are validated before sending input. Background scrolling also needs
+`x,y` to identify the location inside the window. Click a text field before typing
+or using editing keys. `mode:"background"` requires an explicit window; missing or
+stale targets fail instead of falling back to global input.
+
+The pointer overlay cannot take focus or intercept clicks. Targeted screenshots
+include the last virtual pointer position for that window; `show_pointer:false`
+hides the overlay. The native helper exits after each action, so background
+control adds no idle process, timer, or polling loop. Swift is required at build
+time on macOS; the compiled helper is embedded in the shipped binary.
+
+This uses process-directed Quartz events, AppKit event metadata, and the private
+`CGEventSetWindowLocation` symbol resolved at runtime. Missing support produces an
+error. Windows may be covered by other apps, but must remain on the current
+desktop and not minimized. Application support varies: Chrome canvas drawing,
+Unicode text, and ordinary editing keys work in the integration test. Some
+Command-key menu shortcuts are ignored while Chrome is inactive. Games, protected
+surfaces, app dialogs, and other macOS versions need separate validation. There is
+no automatic foreground-input fallback. Desktop actions without a window target
+retain the existing shared-pointer behavior.
+
+Run the native Chrome regression with:
